1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,333
I am James Rosing.

2
00:00:01,333 --> 00:00:07,667
I am a surgeon in Newport Beach, and I
have been getting letters like this one
for fourteen years.

3
00:00:07,667 --> 00:00:11,667
“Not medically necessary.” A payer’s
software writes that in about a second.

4
00:00:11,667 --> 00:00:20,000
Answering it means a physician sits down
with the chart and the payer’s own
coverage rules and writes a point by
point rebuttal by hand.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
That takes hours, so most denials are
never appealed, and the patient absorbs
the cost.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:26,389
This project writes the appeal.

7
00:00:26,389 --> 00:00:28,333
Not a summary and not a template.

8
00:00:28,333 --> 00:00:36,389
A draft the physician reads and signs,
where every sentence is checked against
the patient’s record and the payer’s own
policy by a gate that is not a model.

9
00:00:36,389 --> 00:00:38,333
Here is one of fifty synthetic cases.

10
00:00:38,333 --> 00:00:40,556
No real patient appears anywhere in this
work.

11
00:00:40,556 --> 00:00:45,000
A 47-year-old woman, radiation planning
after lumpectomy for breast cancer,
denied by her Medicare Advantage plan.

12
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,222
Watch what the model does.

13
00:00:47,222 --> 00:01:00,556
Meta Spark 1.3 first asks my coverage
index which version of this plan’s
policy was in force on the date of
service, then opens only the chart
entries it needs.

14
00:01:00,556 --> 00:01:05,000
Each sentence it writes quotes one entry
and names it.

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:14,070
As each sentence arrives, a
deterministic gate checks that the quote
is really in that entry and that every
number, date, and code matches the
source.

16
00:01:14,070 --> 00:01:16,163
Pass or refuse, nothing in between.

17
00:01:16,163 --> 00:01:20,000
Eight of eight verified, in about twelve
seconds, for two cents.

18
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,417
If I change one number in a verified
sentence, the gate refuses it.

19
00:01:25,417 --> 00:01:27,500
That is the whole idea.

20
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:30,000
The model drafts; the record decides.

21
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
And when the chart cannot support an
argument, the model says so.

22
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Here Muse Wonder read all twenty-two
entries for a sepsis criterion, found
nothing, and declined rather than
invent.

23
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:53,136
I ran all fifty cases through three Meta
models, including the open-weight one
hosted on Baseten.

24
00:01:53,136 --> 00:01:54,153
Nine runs.

25
00:01:54,153 --> 00:02:00,254
Agreement with the answer key between
forty-five and fifty out of fifty.

26
00:02:00,254 --> 00:02:01,780
Zero fabricated quotes.

27
00:02:01,780 --> 00:02:06,864
Spark 1.3 did it for under a dollar per
run.

28
00:02:06,864 --> 00:02:15,000
I also injected ninety-five hundred
fabrications into verified sentences to
attack the gate; it caught them.

29
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:29,194
This is what the physician sees: the
appeal, the payer’s denial reason
answered directly, every sentence
footnoted to the entry it quotes.

30
00:02:29,194 --> 00:02:35,000
It stays a draft until a human signs it.

31
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,902
The gate is published as litmus-gate on
npm.

32
00:02:38,902 --> 00:02:42,805
The code and the fifty cases are public.

33
00:02:42,805 --> 00:02:54,024
Next, I put this in front of my own
denials, and then prior authorizations,
which are the same problem in the other
direction.

34
00:02:54,024 --> 00:02:55,000
Thank you.
